## Part 1: Reconstruct

This report evaluates claims in Alfred Lubrano's 2018 article, ["New Census figures on Philly neighborhoods show inequality, high numbers of whites living in poverty"](https://whyy.org/articles/new-census-figures-on-philly-neighborhoods-show-inequality-high-numbers-of-whites-living-in-poverty/). The article describes Philadelphia's 2013–2017 poverty rate as about 26% and reports large increases in several named neighborhoods relative to 2006–2010. Because the article does not publish its tract-to-neighborhood crosswalk, I use census tracts as the unit of analysis and test the broader claim that poverty increased across parts of Philadelphia. I do not attempt the optional neighborhood aggregation.

I use ACS detailed table B17001: `B17001_001` is the total population for whom poverty status is determined (the denominator), and `B17001_002` is the population below the poverty level (the numerator). I use the 2010 and 2017 ACS five-year releases because they correspond exactly to the article's 2006–2010 and 2013–2017 periods and provide tract-level estimates. The periods do **not** overlap: 2006–2010 ends before 2013–2017 begins, with 2011–2012 between them. Treating the estimates as independent is therefore appropriate for the change test, but each value still describes a pooled five-year period rather than a single-year snapshot.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_2010 <- get_acs(
  geography = "tract",
  variables = c(
    total = "B17001_001",
    below_poverty = "B17001_002"
  ),
  state = "PA",
  county = "Philadelphia",
  year = 2010,
  survey = "acs5",
  output = "wide"
)

poverty_2017 <- get_acs(
  geography = "tract",
  variables = c(
    total = "B17001_001",
    below_poverty = "B17001_002"
  ),
  state = "PA",
  county = "Philadelphia",
  year = 2017,
  survey = "acs5",
  output = "wide"
)
```
:::


## Part 2: Test

### Poverty rates and margins of error

The ACS publishes 90% margins of error (MOEs) for the counts. Because a poverty rate is a proportion, I calculate its MOE with `moe_prop()` rather than reusing the MOE for the numerator.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_2010 <- poverty_2010 %>%
  mutate(
    poverty_rate = below_povertyE / totalE,
    poverty_rate_moe = moe_prop(
      num = below_povertyE,
      denom = totalE,
      moe_num = below_povertyM,
      moe_denom = totalM
    )
  )

poverty_2017 <- poverty_2017 %>%
  mutate(
    poverty_rate = below_povertyE / totalE,
    poverty_rate_moe = moe_prop(
      num = below_povertyE,
      denom = totalE,
      moe_num = below_povertyM,
      moe_denom = totalM
    )
  )
```
:::


### Missing values


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
missing_2010 <- poverty_2010 %>%
  filter(
    totalE == 0 |
      is.na(poverty_rate) |
      is.na(poverty_rate_moe)
  )

missing_2017 <- poverty_2017 %>%
  filter(
    totalE == 0 |
      is.na(poverty_rate) |
      is.na(poverty_rate_moe)
  )

nrow(missing_2010)
```

